+\section{Symmetric Taylor maps and coincident supports}
+\label{sec:current-symmetric-taylor}
+
+The quantum differential fails the Leibniz identity when supports overlap.
+Its defect supplies a binary bracket.  The Cauchy conjugation then has
+higher Taylor coefficients which intertwine that bracket with zero.
+At three inputs, a quadratic current already has a nonzero coefficient.
+The same binary coefficient extends the double pole as a distribution
+on the diagonal of the curve.
+
+Fix an open disk $U\subset\C$, the complex orientation, and
+$\int^{\mathrm{res}}=(2\pi i)^{-1}\int$.
+Let $B$ be the graded commutative polynomial algebra of compact smooth
+kernels in $\Omega_c^{0,\bullet}(U)[1]$, with coefficients in
+$\C[\hbar]$.  A function generator has degree $-1$ and a form
+generator has degree zero.  Symmetric powers use graded coinvariants
+of compact smooth kernels on $U^r$.  Their direct sum has finite
+field number.  Write
+\[
+ D=d+\hbar\Delta_\mu,\qquad d=\bar\partial,
+ \qquad \mu(f,v)=-\kappa\int_U^{\mathrm{res}}f\,\partial v.
+\]
+The cocycle is zero except on one function and one form generator.
+Its pair contraction has degree one.  Define
+\[
+ \mathscr C v(z)=\frac1{2\pi i}\int
+                    \frac{v(w)\wedge dw}{z-w},\qquad
+ P(u,v)=-\kappa\int^{\mathrm{res}}(\mathscr C u)\partial v.
+\]
+The pairing $P$ is zero unless both generators are forms.
+Let $K=\Delta_P$ and $N=e^{-\hbar K}$.
+The Cauchy identities give the chain equation
+\[
+ dN=ND.
+\]
+This is Proposition~\ref{prop:bf-cauchy-conjugation}.
+All products in the following formulas are the graded commutative
+products of kernels.  They need not have disjoint supports.
+
+For homogeneous $a,b\in B$, set
+\[
+ \ell_1(a)=Da,\qquad
+ \ell_2(a,b)=D(ab)-(Da)b-(-1)^{|a|}a(Db).
+\]
+Thus $\ell_2$ is the actual second-order Leibniz defect, including
+its factor $\hbar$.  It is graded symmetric and has degree one.
+Use the convention in which an $L_\infty[1]$ structure consists
+of graded symmetric degree-one operations, encoded by a square-zero
+coderivation on the coaugmented symmetric coalgebra.
+Its morphism has graded symmetric Taylor coefficients of degree zero.
+There is no suspension sign hidden in this convention.
+
+For an ordered list of homogeneous inputs $a_1,\ldots,a_n$, let
+$\mathcal P_n$ be its set of partitions into nonempty blocks.
+Order the entries in each block increasingly, and order the blocks
+by their smallest entries.  Let $\epsilon(\pi;a)$ be the Koszul
+sign of this rearrangement.  Put
+\begin{equation}
+\label{eq:current-cumulant-taylor}
+ F_n(a_1,\ldots,a_n)=
+ \sum_{\pi\in\mathcal P_n}
+ (-1)^{|\pi|-1}(|\pi|-1)!\,\epsilon(\pi;a)
+ \prod_{C\in\pi}N\!\left(\prod_{i\in C}a_i\right).
+\end{equation}
+In particular,
+\[
+ F_1=N,\qquad F_2(a,b)=N(ab)-N(a)N(b).
+\]
+
+\begin{theorem}[Supported symmetric Taylor comparison]
+\label{thm:current-supported-taylor}
+The operations $\ell_1,\ell_2$, with $\ell_n=0$ for $n\ge3$,
+define an $L_\infty[1]$ structure on $B$.
+The maps \eqref{eq:current-cumulant-taylor} define an
+$L_\infty[1]$ isomorphism from this structure to the abelian
+complex $(B,d)$.  They are continuous on each fixed compact support
+and fixed polynomial degree and commute with extension by zero.
+Their first higher identity is
+\begin{equation}
+\label{eq:current-binary-taylor-identity}
+ dF_2(a,b)-F_2(Da,b)-(-1)^{|a|}F_2(a,Db)
+                 =N\ell_2(a,b).
+\end{equation}
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+Use the direct sum of finite symmetric words $S^c(B)$, together
+with its coaugmentation.  Each individual word has finite length.
+Let $E$ be the coalgebra map whose Taylor coefficient of every
+positive arity is multiplication.  Its action on a word is the sum
+over partitions of that word, with the stated Koszul signs.
+Its linear part is the identity.  It is triangular in word length,
+so it has a unique coalgebra inverse.
+
+The inverse Taylor coefficient in arity $n$ is
+$(-1)^{n-1}(n-1)!$ times multiplication.  One verifies this by
+substituting partitions: a set with $c$ components contributes
+\[
+ \sum_{\pi\in\mathcal P_c}(-1)^{|\pi|-1}(|\pi|-1)!
+ =\begin{cases}1,&c=1,\\0,&c>1.\end{cases}
+\]
+The identity follows, for example, by comparing coefficients in
+$\log(\exp t)=t$ as formal power series over $\C$.
+Graded reordering supplies precisely the Koszul sign already fixed.
+
+Let $\widehat D$ denote the coderivation applying $D$ to one
+entry of a word.  The conjugate $E^{-1}\widehat D E$ has first
+Taylor coefficient $D$ and second coefficient $\ell_2$.
+Every contraction in $D$ uses at most two input blocks.
+Partition inversion cancels a contribution unless it uses every
+block of its Taylor input.  Thus the coefficients of arity at least
+three vanish.  This argument also applies to the derivation part,
+which uses only one block.  Since $D^2=0$, the conjugate squares to zero.
+This proves the asserted bracket identities with their signs.
+
+The same conjugation for the derivation $d$ has only its unary
+coefficient.  The chain isomorphism $N$ induces the coalgebra
+isomorphism $S^c(N)$.  Therefore
+\[
+ F=E^{-1}S^c(N)E,\qquad
+ \widehat d F=F(E^{-1}\widehat D E).
+\]
+Partition inversion gives exactly
+\eqref{eq:current-cumulant-taylor}.  It also proves every higher
+morphism identity, without an infinite arity sum on a word.
+Its inverse is $E^{-1}S^c(N^{-1})E$.
+Expanding the equation in arity two gives
+\eqref{eq:current-binary-taylor-identity} directly.
+
+For fixed polynomial inputs, all contractions terminate.
+The Cauchy estimate bounds a contracted smooth kernel on its
+compact support by one additional derivative seminorm.
+Every iterated contraction uses disjoint individual field variables.
+Hence it uses tensor products of those continuous contractions,
+not products of distributions in a shared variable.
+Finite sums and ordinary kernel products preserve continuity.
+The global Cauchy formula and zero extension prove naturality.
+\end{proof}
+
+For separated radial current representatives, write $x_i=u_{a_i,0}$
+and $p_{ij}=\kappa(a_i-a_j)^{-2}$.  Then $N(x_i)=x_i$ and
+\[
+ F_2(x_1,x_2)=-\hbar p_{12},\qquad
+ F_3(x_1,x_2,x_3)=0,
+ \qquad F_3(x_1^2,x_2,x_3)=2\hbar^2p_{12}p_{13}.
+\]
+For the last identity, expand
+\[
+\begin{split}
+ N(x_1^2x_2x_3)
+ &=x_1^2x_2x_3
+ -\hbar(2p_{12}x_1x_3+2p_{13}x_1x_2+p_{23}x_1^2)\\
+ &\quad+2\hbar^2p_{12}p_{13}.
+\end{split}
+\]
+The three two-block terms in $F_3$ cancel all single contractions.
+The five uncontracted terms have total coefficient $1-3+2=0$.
+The remaining term joins all three input groups.
+In general, partition inversion retains exactly the matchings whose
+graph on the input groups is connected.  Individual field factors
+still occur in at most one edge.  This explains why three linear
+currents have zero coefficient while a quadratic current changes it.
+
+\begin{proposition}[The distributional collision homotopy]
+\label{prop:current-distributional-collision}
+The binary map on form generators extends the separated double pole
+to all compact smooth two-variable kernels by the distribution
+\[
+ T(z,w)=-\partial_z\frac1{z-w}.
+\]
+It satisfies
+\[
+ \partial_{\bar z}T=-\pi\partial_z\delta^{(2)}(z-w)
+\]
+in coefficient distributions with respect to area measure.
+For a compact function generator $f$ and form generator $v$,
+its Taylor identity is the contact cancellation
+\[
+ F_2(\bar\partial f,v)=-\hbar\mu(f,v),\qquad
+ F_2(f,v)=0,\qquad \ell_2(f,v)=\hbar\mu(f,v).
+\]
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+The function $(z-w)^{-1}$ is locally integrable on $\C^2$.
+Its distributional derivative therefore exists.  For
+$u=a(w)d\bar w$ and $v=b(z)d\bar z$, the actual Cauchy pairing is
+\[
+ P(u,v)=\frac{\kappa}{\pi^2}
+       \int\frac{a(w)\partial_z b(z)}{z-w}\,dA_zdA_w
+       =\frac{\kappa}{\pi^2}\langle T,a(w)b(z)\rangle.
+\]
+The first formula extends to every compact smooth two-variable
+kernel by differentiation in $z$ before integration.
+Away from the diagonal, $T=(z-w)^{-2}$.
+Differentiating
+$\partial_{\bar z}(z-w)^{-1}=\pi\delta^{(2)}(z-w)$
+proves the displayed contact term.
+Adjoining $d\bar z$ gives the corresponding form identity.
+
+On two form generators, $F_2=-\hbar P$.
+On a function and a form, both Cauchy contractions vanish,
+so $F_2(f,v)=0$.  Finally,
+$P(\bar\partial f,v)=\mu(f,v)$ by the compact Cauchy identity.
+These give the contact cancellation and verify
+\eqref{eq:current-binary-taylor-identity} on the first inputs
+where the quantum bracket is nonzero.
+\end{proof}
+
+The proposition concerns smooth test kernels, including overlapping
+supports.  A diagonal distribution need not admit evaluation on
+point-supported distributions.  At nonzero $\kappa$, the scalar
+\[
+ F_2(u_{a,0},u_{b,0})=-\hbar\kappa(a-b)^{-2}
+\]
+has no finite limit as $b\to a$.  The cutoffs must shrink to retain
+disjoint supports, and their smooth seminorms are then unbounded.
+Thus continuity on fixed smooth support spaces does not give a
+collision limit of these point-labelled states.
+
+The maps in Theorem~\ref{thm:current-supported-taylor} refine the
+actual Cauchy chain comparison and retain its quantum differential.
+They supply the symmetric bracket comparison required by the
+supported boundary observables.  The interval operations earlier
+in this chapter are ordered operations on a different coalgebra.
+A morphism from that ordered structure requires ordered Taylor maps
+and their compatibility with the collision chains.
+The symmetric construction does not assert such a morphism.
+Nor does it assign a product to coincident distributional currents.
